== The Text Opened

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Matt 10:41, 42]

    #text(style: "italic")[Whoever receives a #emph[prophet] in the name of a #emph[prophet] will receive a #emph[prophet’s] reward, and whoever receives a righteous man in the name of a righteous man will receive a righteous man’s reward. And if anyone gives even a cup of cold water to one of these little ones in the name of a disciple, truly I tell you#super[+], he will never lose his reward.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] troubles into which God brings his ministers and people are many and great. Yet they are not more, or greater, than the comforts which he gives them to support and encourage them in their troubles. Take this chapter as an instance, and the sermon of Christ recorded in it, concerning the severe trials into which his church and children would be brought. As each kind of affliction is foretold, a fitting consolation of its own is usually joined to it. So, to establish the hearts of his suffering servants and to encourage those who would help them, he closes his sermon with the words of the text. In them he declares the high regard he had, not only for his ministers and people themselves, but also for those who would give them any relief, or show them kindness for his sake. He assures them: “Whoever receives a prophet in the name of a prophet will receive a prophet’s reward,” and “whoever receives a righteous man in the name of a righteous man will receive a righteous man’s reward.”

In brief, the words contain a #emph[declaration of the great benefit which those bring to themselves who give any relief and help to ministers or other saints.] They teach #emph[that there is no such way to fill our treasures as by emptying them for God.]

To clear the words, I will briefly show you#super[+]:

- 1\. Who is meant here by a #emph[prophet,] and who by a #emph[righteous man.]
- 2\. What is meant by #emph[receiving] them.
- 3\. What the #emph[reward] is which follows on it.

I. For the first: who is meant here by a #emph[prophet,] and who by a #emph[righteous] man.

By a #emph[prophet] our Savior here means not only extraordinary ministers, such as were directly and extraordinarily inspired by the #emph[Holy Spirit,] or who had extraordinary revelations to foretell things to come, but also ordinary ministers of the word and interpreters of the Scriptures (John 4:44; Rev 11:18).

By #emph[righteous men] he means his #emph[saints,] that is, those who show their justification through faith in Christ by their sanctification and the fruits of a good conscience. These the Scripture commonly calls #emph[saints.]

II. For the second: what is meant by #emph[receiving these?]

In answer to this, you#super[+] must know that there are two things to which this receiving may refer.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. To the #emph[word] and #emph[message] of a minister. So it means listening to and embracing his holy counsel and doctrine.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. To the #emph[person] of a minister. So it means giving him shelter, or showing him kindness, as is clear from the instance of #emph[cold water] given to a #emph[prophet] or #emph[righteous man,] spoken of in the following verse. Both of these are implied in this phrase, #emph[receiving a prophet:] that is, receiving his #emph[doctrine,] by embracing it and submitting to it, and his #emph[person,] by sheltering, helping and relieving him according to his need.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[By receiving a prophet in the name of a prophet] is meant helping and relieving him #emph[for the sake of his particular calling, eo nomine,] because he is a prophet. So #emph[receiving a righteous man in the name of a righteous man] means the same thing: relieving him #emph[for the sake of his general] calling, because he is a Christian, a child of God and a member of Christ.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[III. For the third particular: what is meant here by #emph[the reward of a prophet, and of a righteous man?] I answer: these phrases, #emph[receiving a prophet’s, and a righteous man’s, reward,] may be taken #emph[actively] or #emph[passively. Actively,] for the reward which a #emph[prophet] or a #emph[righteous man] gives. #emph[Passively,] for the reward which God gives to the one and to the other. #emph[The reward which a prophet gives] is, as a minister, to preach the word of reconciliation to those who receive him, as #emph[Peter] did to #emph[Cornelius] (Acts 10:34, etc.); to pray for them, as #emph[Abraham] did for #emph[Abimelech] (Gen 20:7, 17); and to bless them, as #emph[Melchizedek] blessed #emph[Abraham] (Gen 14:19).]

The reward which a righteous man gives is, in private, to instruct and edify, to pray for, and to be a good pattern and example of righteousness to, those who entertain or otherwise relieve him.

#emph[The reward which is given by God] to either of these, for the kindness anyone shows to them, is so surpassing and excellent a weight of glory that it cannot be expressed by the tongue of men or angels. Yet it differs in degrees, as is clear from Dan 12:3.

Some take these phrases, #emph[receiving a prophet’s] and a #emph[righteous man’s reward,] in the first sense, #emph[actively,] and others #emph[passively.] I think they may be taken in both.

The words, explained in this way, give us several points of doctrine.

From the duty expressed here, of receiving and relieving #emph[prophets] and #emph[righteous men,] and from the way it is set down, we may observe:

1\. #emph[Doctrine.] All needed help and good entertainment are to be given to ministers of the gospel. #emph[This is the main and principal point intended.]

2\. #emph[Doctrine.] Christian kindness is to be shown not only to ministers, but also to all the members of Christ. #emph[For our blessed Savior is not content to have mentioned a] prophet, #emph[but also adds] a righteous man.

These two sorts make up #emph[the household of faith,] to whom the apostle urges Christians #emph[especially] to do good (Gal 6:10).

Q. Is our charity to reach no further?

#emph[A.] Yes, it reaches to all who are in want, but #emph[especially] to those who have a particular relation to Christ, as his members or ministers, because in them Christ himself is helped and relieved in a special way. So it will be our wisdom to take notice of how far this duty reaches. In all we do, whether for Christians or for others, let us see to it that what we do is done for the Lord’s sake and for conscience’ sake: to the one, in compassion to his members; to the other, in obedience to his will.

3\. Doctrine. #emph[According to the kindness which we show, so will our reward be.] He who receives and relieves a #emph[prophet] will receive a #emph[prophet’s reward.] And he who receives and relieves a #emph[righteous man] will receive #emph[the reward of a righteous man.]

Verse 42.

And if anyone gives even a cup of cold water to one of these little ones in the name of a disciple, he will never lose his reward.

Our Savior adds these words to the former to answer beforehand some objections which might be made to them, and also to explain more fully the points mentioned before.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[Obj.] It may be put thus: These preachers of the gospel, and those who profess it, are only lowly persons, and little esteemed. What great reward can be expected for entertaining them?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[A.] Little as they are, be they as insignificant and contemptible as the proud world makes them, yet they are not so little in the eyes of the Lord that he will not reward all who are friendly to them.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Obj.] I myself am only poor, and can do only a little for them.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[A.] Though your kindness is ever so small, as small as a #emph[cup of water,] and that #emph[cold,] taken out of the river without any trouble or cost to heat it, yes, only #emph[ONE] cupful of it and no more (supposing that to be the most you can do for them), yet it will be rewarded by him who regards the willingness of the giver more than the greatness of the gift.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The greatest difficulty in the words is who are meant here by #emph[little ones,] for whose sake the reward is promised.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[By #emph[little ones] our Savior means no others than those whom in the verse before he had called #emph[prophets] and #emph[righteous ones.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[They are called #emph[little ones] in two respects:]]

- 1\. In regard to the world’s esteem of them.
- 2\. In regard to their own estimate of themselves.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, #emph[they are little in the world’s eye.] The world, which does not judge according to inward worth, holds them in low esteem. It goes with the servants as it went with their master: they are despised and rejected and trodden underfoot by men. Of old they were called “Desolate” and “Forsaken” (Isa 62:4). In the #emph[apostles’] time they were “the scum of the earth, the refuse of the world” (1 Cor 4:13). The experience of all ages confirms as much, and among other ages ours gives not the least evidence of it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The comfort and contentment which God’s people find in the Lord and in the light of his countenance, yes, the hope they have of finding grace in his eyes, make them care less for, and seek less after, the honor, wealth and promotions of the world. And commonly they have only a little of it. Now the world judges according to outward, earthly, worldly glory; it does not discern the spiritual glory of the saints (1 John 3:1).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Do not judge men according to the world’s judgment. Do not think any more meanly of the saints because the world thinks meanly of them. They are highly favored by the Most High. They are those #emph[of whom the world is not worthy]\; they are #emph[the excellent ones] (Ps 16:3). But whatever they are in truth, in the eye of the world they are only #emph[little ones.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, #emph[they are little in their own eyes] (Gen 18:27). We read how #emph[Abraham,] speaking to God, calls himself no better than “dust and ashes.” And #emph[Jacob] confesses himself #emph[unworthy,] or #emph[less than the least of God’s mercies] (Gen 32:10).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The reason for this may be that the saints know themselves best. More thoroughly than other men, they search their hearts and observe their ways, and compare themselves with the pure and perfect law of God. They take notice of their inward, secret corruptions as well as of their outward and visible transgressions. In this way they come to know more of themselves than anyone else can know, or even suspect, of them. And this clearer sight of themselves brings them down and lays them low. This is what made Saint #emph[Paul] cry out, “What a wretched man I am! Who will rescue me from this body of death?” (Rom 7:24). This is a mark of true grace and of a sanctified heart. It is the #emph[poor in spirit,] not the proud in spirit, who are Christ’s blessed ones (Matt 5:3). The false disciple thinks highly of himself. He would be counted not ὁ μικρὸς, a #emph[little one,] like a true disciple of Christ, but μέγας τὶς, #emph[someone great,] like #emph[Simon Magus] (Acts 8:8, 9). This spirit is a satanic spirit. Test your spirit by this mark, whether it is of God or not.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But though all the servants of God are #emph[little ones] on the account just given, yet there are some among them less than others. #emph[In regard to their offices] and stations in the church, some are in higher offices and others in lower; some are in office and others not. So also #emph[in regard to their abilities and gifts,] and graces: some are strong and others are weak; some are the chief of the saints, others the least; some are like the head, and others only like the finger or the foot. And it is probable that the text has the most special regard (and its emphasis is the greater) to the least of all these little ones. So the expression is in Matt 25:40: “Whatever you#super[+] did for one #emph[of the least] of these brothers of Mine, you did for Me.”]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I take notice of this all the more to give Christians a hint: in their charitable gifts, let them have an eye to such ministers or Christians as are more obscure and less regarded in the world. It may be that those of greater worth and note taste deeply of their bounty, since almost everyone has a kindness for them. Meanwhile there may be some poor hidden ones whose worth is less but whose wants are greater. Being low in those gifts or graces which would commend them to more public notice, they are almost buried in forgetfulness. It is true that the more eminent the person who is relieved (#emph[caeteris paribus] \[other things being equal\]), the more excellent the work. Yet special care should be taken that the more obscure and least deserving among all who belong to God are not forgotten.]]
